From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Deliver SP and DP edges ahead of retrace

The N64 scheduler's interrupt queue holds eight messages. We deposit one retrace
per frame; the game does not always drain them that fast, and the queue fills.

Measured on Windows 95: the RSP task completion was refused, requeued, and only
deposited nine refusals later. The game does not wait that long -- a few frames
without an answer and its scheduler gives up on the task and clears curRSPTask.
Our message then arrives and __scHandleRSP dereferences a null pointer.

On hardware an SP interrupt and a vertical retrace are independent events whose
relative order is not guaranteed, so serving SP and DP first is faithful. Their
order among themselves is preserved, which is the one the game observes.

Effect: the SP message is now deposited on the first attempt, no requeue. The
crash moves to __scHandleRDP, which is what says the starvation was real -- the
game gets further and meets the next problem.

